---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'Drip vs Postscript for Shopify stores — visual email workflow control vs SMS-native specialization, stack architecture, compliance, and team fit.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Is Drip or Postscript better for a Shopify store doing $80k/mo?',
    answer: 'Revenue alone does not determine the stack. Evaluate email lifecycle ownership and the SMS use cases separately: welcome, cart, post-purchase and winback email, then permissioned text recovery and replies. Verify the current contracted channels and integrations in each account. Choose one or two tools only after checking workflow coverage, staffing and dispatch-time suppression; a revenue milestone is not evidence that both are needed.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can Postscript replace Drip entirely?',
    answer: 'Compare the current contracted product scopes before deciding. Map every email journey, segment and integration you would lose or rebuild, and test it in the destination account. Postscript now markets Email as well as SMS; demonstrate the destination email journeys rather than assuming either complete replacement or SMS-only scope. Do not assume Drip includes a native SMS sender. Establish the actual sender and queue owner for each channel before switching traffic.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How does Drip vs Postscript pricing compare?',
    answer: 'Request current quotes at 12,000 and 15,000 contacts for the actual email and SMS scope. Include destinations, message segments, MMS, carrier fees, integrations and operator time. Compare incremental contribution rather than using attributed SMS sales as proof that a second subscription pays for itself.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I use Drip and Postscript together?',
    answer: 'Use both only when each has a defined job and your team can coordinate them. Document which tool owns each email or SMS step, the permitted purpose and channel, and the current purchase, subscription and suppression state. Cancel queued recovery after purchase or opt-out, and deduplicate event replay using stable event identifiers. Tags alone do not establish SMS permission.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I also consider Sequenzy instead of Drip or Postscript?',
    answer: 'Consider Sequenzy for a defined unmet email requirement, then verify the current integration, queue controls and quote. No automatic cost or maintenance advantage is established by adding a third candidate.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which has better TCPA compliance tooling?',
    answer: 'Evaluate the SMS controls actually available in the contracted account and destination regions: opt-in evidence, opt-out processing, quiet hours, reply handling and audit exports. Do not infer legal compliance from a vendor label or email permission. Current requirements and configuration need review for the proposed message purpose and recipients; product guardrails do not certify a campaign.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which handles cart abandonment better?',
    answer: 'Compare the same eligible cart and offer in the demonstrated accounts. Email can carry product education and SMS can offer a permitted reminder, but neither channel proves faster conversion or incremental recovery. Include purchase during a wait, opt-out, delayed events and reply handling before judging the journey.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What workflow test should decide the winner?',
    answer: 'Build proposed tests rather than preselecting a winner: welcome branched by signup source, a three-step cart journey, and post-purchase cross-sell for email; permissioned recovery, quiet-hour scheduling, keyword opt-in and replies for SMS. Verify current plan and integration support first. Score event accuracy, suppression propagation, queue cancellation, replay deduplication and operator effort with synthetic profiles before any approved live experiment.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-cyan-100/90">/</span>
        <span class="text-cyan-100/90">Drip vs Postscript</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Postscript for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">
          Treat this as a proposed architecture evaluation, not a record of merchant tests. Compare Drip email workflow design with the current Postscript channel offering and the integrations available in your account. A hypothetical streetwear label could test drop alerts alongside email nurture; a supplements shop could map education and recovery; a skincare team could test category-based branching. These scenarios have no verified revenue or observed results. Confirm current product, plan and regional scope before deciding which system owns each journey.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-cyan-100/90">This comparison asks whether to retain a working Drip email program with coordinated Postscript texts or evaluate consolidation into the current Postscript Email offering. The deciding work is who owns the next message when permission, purchase or stock changes during a wait.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Email workflows', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Visual canvas for branching, tags, and behavior triggers.' },
            { label: 'SMS specialization', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'Evaluate SMS controls, reply handling, and Shopify fit.' },
            { label: 'Operator control', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Inspect branching and state with the person maintaining the journey.' },
            { label: 'SMS revenue focus', tool: 'Postscript', reason: 'When text is a primary channel, not an add-on.' },
            { label: 'Also consider', tool: 'Sequenzy', reason: 'Evaluate only for a specific unmet email job.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-white">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Drip</th><th>Postscript</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Visual email workflow operators</td><td>SMS-native Shopify marketing</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Starting price</td><td>Request current scope quote</td><td>Request email/SMS scope quote</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Email</td><td>Email workflow candidate</td><td>Email offering now available; demonstrate required paths</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SMS depth</td><td>Verify current sender and integration scope</td><td>Evaluate current Shopify SMS scope</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Workflow UI</td><td>Visual canvas</td><td>SMS automations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Compliance</td><td>Review configured email permission and suppression</td><td>Review configured SMS permission, replies and scheduling</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shopify depth</td><td>Inspect required Shopify events and fields</td><td>Inspect required Shopify events and fields</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical role</td><td>Primary email ESP</td><td>SMS layer in combined stack</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Two viable architectures, with current product scope</h2>
        <p>Drip's <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702569357-Getting-Started-with-Workflows">workflow guide</a> documents triggers and filters, including Shopify order-created entry. This supports a positive fit for an operator maintaining explicit email branches. Postscript's <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/email">Email offering</a> means consolidation deserves consideration alongside keeping Drip. Demonstrate templates, entry rules, exclusions and reporting in the proposed account before retiring working email flows.</p>
        <p>Postscript's <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13564366-what-is-my-phone-number-subscribers-can-text-to-opt-into-my-sms">number guide</a> documents subscriber replies to a brand number. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563939-international-waking-hours">waking-hours guide</a> describes automation deferral to the next waking window. Those are useful SMS capabilities; they also create a staffing task and a delayed-dispatch acceptance case. Documentation reviewed November 25, 2026.</p>
        <h2>Why this comparison matters for Shopify merchants</h2>
        <p>
          Drip and Postscript appear in retention-stack comparisons, but tool labels do not define the current contracted scope. Start with a journey map: email education, browse nurture, permissioned text alerts and reply handling. In a hypothetical streetwear shop, test whether separate tools give the operator useful control over post-purchase education and drop announcements. Do not presume that speed or a channel label predicts conversion.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a proposed supplements-shop evaluation, inventory welcome, post-purchase cross-sell and winback alongside cart recovery and keyword acquisition. Check that the intended destination supports each required channel, trigger and exclusion. Rehearse exports, field mappings and suppression propagation before switching; there is no verified eight-week migration or measured outcome behind this example.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: welcome and onboarding</h2>
        <p>
          For the welcome test, propose branches for popup versus footer signup and first product viewed. Verify that the required events, identifiers and integration actions exist in the current plan. Test SMS handoff only for profiles with permission for the intended text purpose; a tag is not consent. Record operator effort and event latency instead of promising an afternoon setup or declaring a winner from an assumed revenue mix.
        </p>
        <p>
          A hypothetical skincare team could make welcome email three reflect the first category viewed and invite a separate text opt-in for restock alerts. Verify category-event coverage and field mapping, then test whether permission and suppression propagate correctly. Assign the sending tool, queue owner and cancellation responsibility before seasonal changes. This is an evaluation scenario, not an observed successful handoff.
        </p>

        <h2>Workflow test: abandoned cart</h2>
        <p>
          Proposed streetwear fixture: compare education with a capped 10% offer in step two, using an explicit discount-sensitivity field rather than assuming a tag predicts response. Include a shopper already in winback. Inspect the branch and exclusion in each configured sender; no faster text delivery or tighter Drip suppression has been measured here.        </p>
        <p>
          For the proposed streetwear test, assign email steps one and three and an optional text step two only to shoppers permitted to receive that purpose and channel. Recheck purchase, subscription and suppression state immediately before dispatch. Document which sender cancels queued messages and how retries or replayed events are deduplicated; a shared list alone does not prove that cancellation has happened.
        </p>

        <h2>Pricing reality for combined stacks</h2>
        <p>
          Quote 12,000 and 15,000 contacts with normal and BFCM sending volumes. For the streetwear example, model three proposed broadcast tiers and explicit spending alerts or caps supported by the account. Include email migration labor if evaluating Postscript Email and integration monitoring if retaining Drip. A 20% attributed recovery share at $95 AOV does not establish incremental profit.
        </p>
        <p>Reconcile delivered and billed message counts by destination and encoding, plus carrier fees, discounts and fixed subscriptions. Model twelve-month contact growth and any overlap while old queues drain. A combined stack is justified only when its incremental contribution or operational benefit exceeds its added cost.</p>

        <h2>Shopify data depth in production</h2>
        <p>
          Evaluate Shopify event and field coverage with synthetic orders, customer tags and product changes. Check identifiers, delay semantics and regional or plan restrictions in each current integration. Then verify that each event reaches the intended workflow once, that opt-out propagates, and that a purchase cancels recovery still in the queue. Treat complementary channel roles as a hypothesis to validate, not evidence that every store needs two systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a hypothetical skincare catalog with replenishment intervals from 28 to 60 days, test product-specific delays and purchase exclusions using synthetic profiles. Restock subscription state and marketing permission need separate checks for the intended text. Compare education and urgency scenarios without asserting that a particular split performed better; the examples describe proposed tests rather than measured results.
        </p>

        <h2>Team fit: who should choose which</h2>
        <p><strong>Choose Drip if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your retention owner thinks in workflows and wants canvas visibility for email</li>
          <li>Custom branching by tags, custom fields, and purchase behavior is central</li>
          <li>You will A/B test email journey paths frequently</li>
          <li>SMS is secondary — or you will pair a dedicated SMS tool like Postscript</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Choose Postscript if:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your planned SMS use cases justify dedicated ownership after a matched evaluation</li>
          <li>Two-way conversations, keyword campaigns, and TCPA guardrails matter</li>
          <li>Drop-day urgency and mobile-first flash catalogs define your channel mix</li>
          <li>Email already lives in Drip, Klaviyo, or another ESP — Postscript is the SMS layer</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Dispatch ownership: a handoff must be acknowledged</h2>
        <p>For the supplements fixture, assign email steps one and three to Drip and optional SMS step two to Postscript, then compare that with a demonstrated consolidated journey. Record recipient, checkout, message purpose, event key, intended dispatch time and the responsible queue. An upstream request to suppress is not proof that a downstream waiting message was cancelled.</p>
        <p>Exercise a purchase during quiet hours before a deferred text, a withdrawn phone subscription, a changed cart variant and a sold-out drop. Record the cancellation acknowledgment or operator fallback. Replaying a checkout must not create a second reminder; a late purchase event must not restart recovery. Set failure handling for missing acknowledgments before routing traffic across tools.</p>
        <p>For keyword acquisition, retain the exact keyword disclosure separately from popup and footer consent. For ordinary replies, assign an operator and escalation path for sizing, shipping and supplement-use questions; STOP/HELP processing must remain distinct from those conversations. Set response expectations the actual team can meet.</p>
        <h2>Also consider Sequenzy</h2>
        <p>Shortlist it only against a specific email workflow that remains unmet. Verify required events, branching, cancellation and current pricing rather than assuming a leaner stack or lower maintenance.</p>
        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>Retain Drip when its demonstrated email logic and operator visibility justify it. Postscript is a positive SMS candidate for staffed keyword and reply journeys, and its Email offering creates a consolidation path to evaluate. Choose between those architectures using the same welcome, cart and replenishment fixtures, dispatch evidence and total cost; store revenue alone does not mandate two tools.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>Dispatch checklist and contribution model</h2>
      <p>Before enabling SMS, inspect live popup, checkout and keyword disclosures. Retain source, wording, timestamp, purpose and withdrawal evidence; review destination and plan coverage, local quiet hours and promised weekly frequency. Email subscription does not establish permission for a text. Assign an owner to ordinary replies, STOP/HELP processing and support escalation; email unsubscribe and SMS withdrawal require channel-specific handling.</p>
      <p>Review opt-outs per flow, reply backlog, stale queued reminders and recent purchases or subscriptions. Choose channel spacing from the customer promise and message purpose, rather than a universal twenty-four-hour rule. Keep education and product context in the email journey when useful, and justify each additional text.</p>
      <p>Illustrative economics: a $100 order with $45 product cost and $10 fulfillment leaves $45 before messaging and platform costs. A 25% discount leaves $20, from which three texts, carrier charges and allocated fees must still be deducted. The discount alone does not prove negative margin. Compare incremental contribution with an eligible randomized holdout where feasible, reporting uncertainty, overlapping exposures and full-price repeat orders separately from dashboard attribution.</p>
      <p>Archive the configured journey, event samples, cancellation evidence, quote date and operator time. Trial length should follow the purchase cycle and available eligible sample, not an arbitrary migration deadline. These are proposed acceptance checks, not tests performed for this article.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
